The OP was labeled "arrogant" and "rude" for posting a friendly
question in an everyday manner.  I can't believe that any adult would
need this explained to them.


>It's arrogant and/or rude to post to a newsgroup without learning how
>first.
>
>It's arrogant and/or rude to post to a newsgroup without regard
>for proper netiquette.


The OP does know how to post to this newsgroup, rather intelligently
IMO, and he is observing netiquette.  Raisse is not.

Rather than assuming a negative, it could just be that most people
nowadays don't expect that, in an internet message forum, there would
be an extremely nitpicky set of posting format restrictions, and that
they would be so avidly enforced by anyone.  In very, very few venues
for informal discussion, on the internet or otherwise, is so much
attention paid to syntax by some people before any is given to
content.

On top of this, the structure of Usenet is such that newsgroup
guidelines (and I stress the word part "guide") are not guaranteed to
be presented to a new user before they want to post.  The user has to
actively search, and first has to KNOW that they have to actively
search.  This is a defective design for presenting important
information, and thus a poor method for steering discussion.  Web
sites do it much better.  Usenet was created, in earlier, different
days of the internet, for a more homogeneous set of users who more or
less understood the rules of a different game.

In light of all this, the kind of hostile policing displayed by Raisse
is out of line.  As it would be out of line in most situations.


>If you're telling the truth about this, then you need to learn to lurk
